y value\22, params[i].value) {\0A            Err(e) => Err(e),\0A            Ok(_) => validate_params(params, i + 1),\0A        },\0A    } }\0A\0A/// `a=1&b=2` into pairs, still encoded. A field with no `=` has an\0A/// empty value (`?flag`), and an empty field is skipped (`?a=1&&b=2`)\0A/// rather than becoming a nameless pair.\0Alet parse_params_acc (fields: Array[String]) (i: Int) (acc: Array[Param]): Array[Param] =\0A    if i >= fields.len() { acc }\0A    else if String.is_empty(fields[i]) { parse_params_acc(fields, i + 1, acc) }\0A    else {\0A        let kv = split_first(fields[i], \22=\22);\0A        parse_params_acc(fields, i + 1, acc.push(Param { name: kv.before, value: kv.after }))\0A    }\0A\0Alet raw_params (raw_query: String): Array[Param] =\0A    if String.is_empty(raw_query) { [] }\0A    else { parse_params_acc(String.split(raw_query, \22&\22), 0, []) }\0A\0A/// The authority, minus any `[...]` IPv6 brackets, plus a port.\0A///\0A/// Brackets are STRIPPED rather than kept: `Net.connect_to` passes the\0A/// host to `getaddrinfo`, which wants `::1` and not `[::1]`. They\0A/// exist in the URL only to keep the colons in an IPv6 address from\0A/// being read as a port separator, which is a syntax problem and not\0A/// something the host itself carries.\0Alet parse_authority (authority: String) (scheme: String): Result[Url, String] =\0A    if String.contains(authority, \22@\22) {\0A        Err(\22Url.parse: userinfo (user@host) is not supported\22)\0A    } else if String.is_empty(authority) {\0A        Err(\22Url.parse: missing host\22)\0A    } else if String.starts_with(authority, \22[\22) {\0A        match String.index_of(authority, \22]\22) {\0A            None => Err(\22Url.parse: unterminated IPv6 address\22),\0A            Some(close) => {\0A                let host = String.slice(authority, 1, close);\0A                let rest = String.slice(authority, close + 1, clen(authority));\0A                if String.is_empty(rest) { Ok(host_port(scheme, ho